markOop: stop clang from assuming the mark word's tag bits are zero

markOop is a tagged pseudo-pointer: the low three bits of the "pointer" are
not address bits, they are the lock/biased pattern (locked_value = 0,
unlocked_value = 1, monitor_value = 2, marked_value = 3).  value() casts
this to uintptr_t to get at them.

clang 16 and later assume that a pointer to markOopDesc is aligned to
alignof(markOopDesc), so those bits are known to be zero and the tag tests
fold away.  With the zero/bionic build the folding was visible directly:
for a neutral mark word of 1, has_locker() returned true and is_neutral()
returned false, which sent inflate() down the stack-locked path and made
it dereference "1 & ~monitor_value" -- fault addr 0x1.  The same folding
in DefNewGeneration::copy_to_survivor_space() produced a wild forwardee
pointer on the first young GC.  Compiled at -O0 both paths are correct,
which is what pinned it to the optimizer rather than to the sources.

Launder the cast through an empty asm so the value stays opaque to the
optimizer; the tag bits are then visible again in every inlined predicate
and the whole VM can go back to being optimised.

jdk 11 replaced markOop with a value-type mark word, which is the
structural fix for the same problem; on jdk 8 the laundering above is the
whole change.

diff --git a/hotspot/src/share/vm/oops/markOop.hpp b/hotspot/src/share/vm/oops/markOop.hpp
index 649217c..ccb1dd4 100644
--- a/hotspot/src/share/vm/oops/markOop.hpp
+++ b/hotspot/src/share/vm/oops/markOop.hpp
@@ -104,7 +104,18 @@ class JavaThread;
 class markOopDesc: public oopDesc {
  private:
   // Conversion
-  uintptr_t value() const { return (uintptr_t) this; }
+  // markOop is a tagged pseudo-pointer whose low three
+  // bits carry the lock/biased pattern, but clang 16+ assumes a pointer to this
+  // class is aligned to alignof(markOopDesc) and folds the tag tests in
+  // has_locker(), is_neutral(), has_monitor() and has_displaced_mark_helper() as if
+  // those bits were always zero (observed: a neutral mark word of 1 made
+  // has_locker() true and is_neutral() false in the android Zero build).
+  // Launder the value through an empty asm so the low bits stay visible.
+  uintptr_t value() const {
+    uintptr_t v = (uintptr_t) this;
+    __asm__ __volatile__ ("" : "+r" (v));
+    return v;
+  }
 
  public:
   // Constants
